{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfc49C24S3ASqFA6Br1o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Vanadate contains V(V), which is d⁰. It has no d electrons, so it has no d–d transitions. The Tanabe–Sugano and ligand-field framework (Dq, B) does not apply to it.\n\nThe three bands, at 37 210, 44 860 and 52 370 cm⁻¹, are intense, with ε of about 2 700 to 9 600 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹. That is far too strong for Laporte-forbidden d–d bands in an octahedral complex, which usually have ε below about 100. In tetrahedral complexes d–d bands are stronger, but not to this extent. These bands are ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) transitions from O 2p (t₁, t₂) orbitals to the empty V 3d (e, t₂) orbitals. They are typically assigned as 1t₁→2e, 1t₁→2t₂ and the 2t₂→2e/t₂ series.\n\nThe splitting between the e and t₂ acceptor levels is roughly 44 860 − 37 210 ≈ 7 650 cm⁻¹ (and 52 370 − 44 860 ≈ 7 510 cm⁻¹). This is only a rough estimate of the tetrahedral splitting, Δt, from the CT bands. It is not a ligand-field Dq. The Racah B has no meaning here, because there are no d–d electron repulsion terms in a d⁰ ion. Any Dq or B fitted to these bands would be an artifact, so I am not reporting values.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"VO4^3- is d0 and its intense bands are O→V charge-transfer, not d-d transitions, so Dq and B cannot be determined.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":253,"cache_read_input_tokens":7806,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8cd61bc2-305f-49ba-a735-f6e2b94f7348","uuid":"e7f7b2d5-e054-407a-b051-5a1238086746","ti